  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nA trough moving across northern portions of the CONUS begins to \nflatten out the ridge Tuesday, and by the latter half of the week \nthe center of the ridge is forced westward again. Temperatures \nchange very little across west Texas and southeast New Mexico as \nthis occurs. Highs remain in the mid-to-upper 90s for most locations \nthrough the week, with upper 80s and low 90s in the higher terrain. \nMeanwhile, low rain chances (10-30%) become more confined to the \nhigher terrain west of the Pecos River. Going into the weekend, \nmodels have come into a bit better agreement. Both the GFS and Euro \nnow depict the ridge becoming dampened by an incoming trough and \nthen being pushed east of our area by early next week. This would \ncause heights to gradually decrease over the region, and therefore \nresult in at least slightly cooler temperatures by the end of the \nweekend/ beginning of next week. At this time, low rain chances (10-\n30%) remain mainly in/near the higher terrain over the weekend, \nthough decreasing subsidence may allow for additional rain chances \nelsewhere. Details will become clearer in future forecast updates, \nso stay tuned!\n\nSprang",
